/* AI System Design — 46 questions */
window.QA_SECTION = {
  id: '07',
  slug: 'system-design',
  title: 'AI System Design',
  blurb: 'End-to-end architectures, tradeoffs, capacity planning, and the failure modes that surface at scale.',
  questions: [
{ q: 'Design ChatGPT: training to serving, end to end.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Training pipeline.</strong> Pretraining: trillions of tokens, deduplicated and quality-filtered, next-token prediction on a decoder-only Transformer across thousands of GPUs with 3D parallelism (data, tensor, pipeline). Then SFT on curated instruction demonstrations. Then preference alignment — reward model on human comparisons plus PPO, or DPO for lower complexity. Safety training throughout.<br><br><strong>Serving architecture.</strong> Gateway (auth, rate limit, quota) → moderation on input → router by model tier → inference cluster → moderation on output → stream to client.<br><br><strong>The inference layer is where the interesting engineering lives.</strong> Prefill and decode have opposite bottlenecks: prefill is compute-bound and sets TTFT, decode is memory-bandwidth-bound and sets tokens/sec. Modern stacks <em>disaggregate</em> them onto separate GPU pools so each is sized independently. Continuous batching keeps the GPU saturated by admitting new requests at each decode step rather than waiting for the slowest sequence. PagedAttention eliminates KV-cache fragmentation and enables prefix sharing across requests. Prompt caching reuses the KV cache for common system prompts.<br><br><strong>State:</strong> conversation history in a fast store, retrieved per turn; the model itself is stateless.<br><br><strong>SLOs to state:</strong> TTFT p95 under ~500ms, inter-token latency under ~50ms (faster than reading speed), availability 99.9%+.<br><br><strong>The tradeoff to name explicitly:</strong> throughput and latency are in tension. Larger batches raise GPU utilization and lower cost per token, but queue delay raises TTFT. Serving economics is choosing that operating point per tier — and it is why cheap tiers batch harder than premium ones.` },

{ q: 'Design a RAG system (chat with your documents).', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Ingestion pipeline (offline):</strong> upload → parse (layout-aware for PDFs; this is the quality ceiling) → chunk on structural boundaries with overlap → embed → write vectors plus metadata plus original text. Content-hash for incremental updates; store <code>document_id</code> so updates are an atomic delete-then-insert.<br><br><strong>Query pipeline (online):</strong> rewrite the query using conversation history (essential for follow-ups — "what about the other one?" is unretrievable alone) → hybrid retrieval (dense + BM25, fused with RRF) with a mandatory permission filter → cross-encoder rerank ~50 candidates to top 5 → assemble prompt with best chunks at the start and end → generate with citations → verify cited spans exist.<br><br><strong>The components that separate a working system from a demo:</strong> reranking (highest ROI), hybrid search (fixes exact-match queries), query rewriting (fixes multi-turn), and an abstention threshold on the reranker score so weak retrieval refuses rather than hallucinates.<br><br><strong>Scale and multi-tenancy:</strong> namespace per tenant; permissions as a hard pre-filter derived from the authenticated session, never from model output. Cache keyed by tenant.<br><br><strong>Evaluation, designed in from the start:</strong> measure retrieval (recall@k, context precision) and generation (faithfulness, answer relevance) <em>separately</em> — an end-to-end score tells you it is broken, not which half.<br><br><strong>The failure to design against:</strong> silent staleness. Deleted documents keep answering because their chunks were never removed. Monitor index age and assert chunk counts after updates.` },

{ q: 'Design memory for a personal AI assistant.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>The core design decision: separate memory types by access pattern, not by age.</strong> This is what most implementations get wrong.<br><br><strong>Structured facts store</strong> — durable attributes: name, timezone, dietary restrictions, family members, preferences, ongoing projects. Key-value or a small schema, <em>always injected</em> regardless of relevance. This must not be a vector store: "user is allergic to penicillin" retrieved only when semantically similar to the query is a critical failure. Extract these with an LLM pass after each session, with conflict resolution favoring recency.<br><br><strong>Episodic store</strong> — session summaries and notable events, in a vector index, retrieved by semantic similarity to the current conversation.<br><br><strong>Working context</strong> — recent turns verbatim plus a rolling summary of the current session.<br><br><strong>Retrieval per turn:</strong> all structured facts + top-k episodic memories + recent turns. Budget each explicitly.<br><br><strong>The hard problems worth raising:</strong> <em>updating</em> — "I moved to Berlin" must supersede the old city, so facts need versioning and conflict resolution rather than append-only accumulation. <em>Forgetting</em> — memory that only grows becomes expensive and noisy; decay or archive stale episodic memories. <em>Compounding loss</em> — recursive summarization drifts, so keep extracted facts verbatim and summarize from originals.<br><br><strong>Privacy is a first-class design constraint:</strong> users must be able to view, edit, and delete memories. That requires a real store with per-item deletion — a strong argument against putting anything user-specific into weights.` },

{ q: 'Design a deep research agent.', tags: [], a: `<strong>Architecture:</strong> a planner decomposes the question into sub-questions → parallel search subagents each investigate one thread in an isolated context → a synthesizer combines findings into a cited report.<br><br><strong>The subagent design is the key move, and the reason is context economics, not specialization.</strong> A research thread may consume 60k tokens reading sources and return a 1k-token summary with citations. The orchestrator's context stays clean, threads run in parallel, and each subagent gets a focused tool set. Give each a self-contained brief — it starts cold.<br><br><strong>Tools:</strong> web search, page fetch with content extraction, an internal document index, and code execution for quantitative work.<br><br><strong>Quality controls, which is where these systems live or die:</strong> require citations for every claim and verify the cited text actually exists on the page. Cross-check contentious claims across independent sources. Track source credibility and recency. Explicitly surface disagreement between sources rather than blending it into a confident synthesis. Detect and report gaps — "I could not find data on X" is a valuable output.<br><br><strong>Budgets are mandatory:</strong> per-subagent step and token caps, plus a <em>global</em> budget at the orchestrator — per-agent caps sum to unbounded under fan-out.<br><br><strong>Latency:</strong> minutes, not seconds. Design for it — stream progress ("searching…", "found 12 sources, analyzing"), checkpoint state so long runs resume, and let users see partial results.<br><br><strong>The failure mode to name:</strong> error compounding. A confidently wrong subagent finding is consumed as fact by the synthesizer. Pass confidence and provenance with every result, and have the synthesizer flag single-source claims.` },

{ q: 'Design a multi-agent customer support system.', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Start by questioning the premise:</strong> most support workloads do not need multiple agents. A single agent with RAG over the knowledge base, a few account tools, and good escalation handles the large majority — and is cheaper, faster, and far easier to debug. Multi-agent earns its place when tool sets and permissions genuinely differ by domain (billing versus technical versus account security), because scoping tools per agent improves selection and limits blast radius.<br><br><strong>Architecture:</strong> a router classifies intent → routes to a specialist agent (billing, technical, account) → each has scoped tools and its own knowledge namespace → escalation path to human agents.<br><br><strong>The router is the highest-leverage component.</strong> A cheap classifier handling routing well beats an expensive orchestrator reasoning about it. Measure routing accuracy separately.<br><br><strong>Escalation triggers, all of them:</strong> explicit request (immediate, no negotiation), low retrieval confidence, sentiment/frustration detection, repeated failure over N turns, policy tier (refunds above a limit, account closure, legal/safety topics), and out-of-scope classification.<br><br><strong>Handoff quality is where implementations fail:</strong> pass the full transcript, retrieved context, attempted actions, and a summary of the customer's goal. An escalation that makes the customer repeat themselves is worse than no agent.<br><br><strong>Guardrails:</strong> authorization on every account action against the authenticated customer, hard refund limits, confirmation before irreversible actions, PII redaction in logs.<br><br><strong>Measure:</strong> resolution rate, escalation rate <em>and appropriateness</em> (false escalations and missed ones), CSAT split by path, and groundedness on a sampled audit.` },

{ q: 'Design an on-device AI assistant.', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>The constraints drive everything:</strong> a few GB of RAM, no sustained high power draw (thermal throttling and battery), NPU/GPU with limited memory bandwidth, and no guaranteed network.<br><br><strong>Model:</strong> a 1–4B parameter SLM, quantized to 4-bit (int4/NF4), roughly 0.5–2GB. Use GQA to shrink the KV cache — on-device, memory bandwidth is the binding constraint on tokens/sec. Runtimes: Core ML / MLX on Apple, ONNX Runtime, llama.cpp, MediaPipe.<br><br><strong>The architecture that actually works is hybrid routing:</strong> an on-device classifier decides per request whether to answer locally or escalate to cloud. Local handles the high-frequency, latency-sensitive, privacy-sensitive cases — intent classification, summarizing local content, simple Q&A, on-device data lookup. Cloud handles complex reasoning and broad world knowledge. This is not a compromise; it is the design, because it gives you privacy and offline capability on the common path and frontier quality on the tail.<br><br><strong>What on-device buys:</strong> data never leaves the device (a genuine compliance and trust argument), no per-token cost, no network latency, works offline.<br><br><strong>What it costs:</strong> materially lower capability, model updates gated on app releases, device fragmentation (you must degrade gracefully on older hardware), and a large app binary.<br><br><strong>Practical details:</strong> lazy-load the model, unload under memory pressure, cap generation length to protect battery, and use speculative decoding with a tiny draft model where the runtime supports it.<br><br><strong>Design the fallback explicitly:</strong> what happens on an old device with 3GB RAM and no network is a product decision, not an error case.` },

{ q: 'Design a multimodal search system (text, image, video).', tags: [], a: `<strong>The key decision: unified embedding space versus per-modality indexes with text as the bridge.</strong><br><br><strong>Recommended: text-bridged.</strong> At ingest, run a vision-language model over each image and video keyframe to produce rich text descriptions, plus extract OCR text, transcripts (ASR for audio), and metadata. Embed all of it with a strong <em>text</em> embedder into one index. Retrieval stays textual, so you keep hybrid search, BM25, and reranking — all of which are far more mature than multimodal retrieval.<br><br><strong>Why not CLIP-style unified embeddings as the primary path:</strong> they were trained on short captions and are much weaker than text embedders for document-style retrieval, with no lexical arm to compensate. Keep a CLIP index as a <em>secondary</em> arm for genuine visual similarity queries ("find images that look like this"), fused with RRF.<br><br><strong>Video specifically:</strong> segment into scenes, extract keyframes, transcribe audio, and index at segment granularity with timestamps so results deep-link to the moment. Indexing whole videos as single units makes them unfindable and unusable.<br><br><strong>Serving:</strong> return the artifact plus the matched span; rerank across modalities with a cross-encoder over the text representations.<br><br><strong>The cost to plan for:</strong> ingest is expensive — a VLM call per image and per keyframe, ASR per video. This is a batch pipeline with checkpointing, not an inline operation, and it dominates the system's cost profile.<br><br><strong>Evaluation:</strong> per-modality recall, since aggregate numbers hide that video search is broken while image search works.` },

{ q: 'Design an LLM inference platform (vLLM-as-a-service).', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Layers:</strong> API gateway (auth, quota, rate limit) → request router → model-specific worker pools → GPU nodes running vLLM/SGLang/TensorRT-LLM.<br><br><strong>The core scheduling insight: prefill and decode have opposite bottlenecks.</strong> Prefill is compute-bound and sets TTFT; decode is memory-bandwidth-bound and sets throughput. Naive interleaving means a long prefill stalls every in-flight decode, spiking inter-token latency. Solutions: <em>chunked prefill</em> (split long prompts and interleave with decode), or <em>disaggregated serving</em> (separate GPU pools for each phase, transferring KV cache between them) which lets you size and scale each independently.<br><br><strong>Memory management is the other half:</strong> PagedAttention stores the KV cache in fixed-size blocks like OS virtual memory, eliminating the fragmentation that otherwise wastes 60–80% of cache capacity, and enabling copy-on-write prefix sharing across requests with common system prompts. Continuous batching admits and evicts sequences at each decode step, keeping the GPU saturated under variable-length generation.<br><br><strong>Multi-tenancy:</strong> multi-LoRA serving — one base model with per-request adapter selection — so hundreds of fine-tuned variants share GPUs. Priority queues and per-tenant quotas prevent one customer starving others.<br><br><strong>Autoscaling is hard here:</strong> GPU cold starts are minutes (loading tens of GB of weights), so you cannot scale reactively. Keep warm pools, scale on queue depth with lead time, and admission-control rather than accepting unbounded queues.<br><br><strong>SLOs:</strong> TTFT p95, inter-token latency p95, throughput (tokens/sec/GPU), and goodput — requests meeting SLO, which is the metric that actually matters.` },

{ q: 'Design an LLM evaluation platform.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Core objects:</strong> datasets (versioned sets of test cases), evaluators (metrics), runs (a config executed against a dataset), and comparisons between runs. Everything versioned, because the point is detecting change.<br><br><strong>Evaluator types, layered:</strong><br>• <em>Deterministic</em> — exact match, F1, schema validity, regex, code execution. Fast, free, reliable. Use wherever possible.<br>• <em>Model-graded</em> — LLM judges with rubrics for open-ended output. Necessary but must be <em>calibrated against human labels</em> before you trust them; an uncalibrated judge is a random number generator with good prose. Prefer pairwise comparison over absolute 1–10 scoring, and randomize position to control for position bias.<br>• <em>Human</em> — sampled review, and the ground truth that validates the judges.<br><br><strong>What the platform must provide beyond running metrics:</strong> statistical rigor — multiple runs per case with variance reported, not a single number, since LLM output is non-deterministic. Regression detection with significance testing, so you do not chase noise. Cost and latency tracked alongside quality, because a 2% quality gain at 3x cost is usually a bad trade.<br><br><strong>Integration is what makes it used:</strong> CI gates on prompt, model, and retrieval changes; trace linkage so a production failure becomes a test case in one click; and dataset curation from production logs, oversampling failures.<br><br><strong>The organizational point:</strong> the hardest part is not the infrastructure, it is getting teams to define what "good" means precisely enough to measure. A platform without curated datasets and validated judges produces confident, meaningless numbers.` },

{ q: 'Design a text-to-image generation service.', tags: [], a: `<strong>Architecture:</strong> API → moderation on the prompt → job queue → GPU worker pool running a diffusion model → moderation on the output image → object storage + CDN → webhook/poll for completion.<br><br><strong>Async by design.</strong> Generation takes seconds to tens of seconds, so this is a job system, not a request/response API. Return a job id immediately, stream progress, and deliver via CDN. Trying to serve this synchronously is the most common architectural mistake.<br><br><strong>GPU economics dominate.</strong> Batch requests aggressively — diffusion batches well since every image runs the same number of denoising steps. Offer quality tiers by step count (20 steps fast, 50 steps high quality) and resolution; steps map almost linearly to cost. Use a distilled/turbo model for the fast tier. Cache aggressively: identical prompt + seed + params is deterministic, so it is a pure cache hit.<br><br><strong>Queue management:</strong> priority tiers, per-user concurrency limits, and admission control — under load, reject or degrade rather than growing an unbounded queue where everyone waits 10 minutes.<br><br><strong>Safety is a first-class requirement, not an add-on:</strong> prompt classification (blocked concepts), output image classification (NSFW, violence), and — importantly — likeness and trademark handling. Log prompts for abuse investigation, rate-limit, and keep a human review path.<br><br><strong>The second-order concern:</strong> provenance. Attach C2PA metadata or watermarks so generated images are identifiable downstream. This is increasingly a regulatory expectation, and retrofitting it is far harder than designing it in.` },

{ q: 'Design a music generation service.', tags: [], a: `<strong>Pipeline:</strong> text prompt (and optional lyrics/reference audio) → generate an intermediate representation → decode to waveform → post-process (loudness normalization, fades) → encode to delivery formats → CDN.<br><br><strong>Model approach:</strong> most systems generate discrete audio tokens autoregressively (an audio codec like EnCodec/SoundStream compresses waveform into tokens, a Transformer models the token sequence, the codec decoder reconstructs audio), or use latent diffusion over a spectrogram/latent representation. The codec-token approach dominates because it turns audio generation into a sequence modeling problem the whole LLM stack already solves.<br><br><strong>The hard technical constraint is duration.</strong> A 3-minute track at typical codec rates is a very long token sequence, so you need hierarchical generation (coarse structure first, then fine detail) or chunked generation with overlap and cross-fading. Naive long-sequence generation drifts — the outro sounds unrelated to the intro — so global structure conditioning matters.<br><br><strong>Serving:</strong> async job queue like any GPU-bound generative service. Generation takes tens of seconds to minutes; stream partial results if the architecture allows.<br><br><strong>The dominant risk is legal, not technical.</strong> Training data provenance, voice likeness (generating a recognizable artist's voice), and melodic similarity to copyrighted works are active litigation areas. Design in: an artist-name blocklist in prompts, output similarity checks against a reference catalog, clear licensing terms on outputs, and provenance metadata. A team that treats this as a post-launch concern is building a liability.` },

{ q: 'Design a video generation service.', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>The defining constraint is cost.</strong> Video is the most expensive generative modality by a wide margin — a few seconds can take minutes of GPU time. Every design decision follows from that.<br><br><strong>Model approach:</strong> latent diffusion with a spatiotemporal architecture (DiT-style), generating in a compressed latent space then decoding to frames. Temporal consistency is the core technical problem — objects must persist coherently across frames — handled with 3D attention or temporal layers, plus conditioning on the whole clip rather than frame-by-frame generation.<br><br><strong>Architecture:</strong> strictly async job queue with long-running GPU workers, progress streaming, and checkpointed intermediate results. Multi-stage: generate at low resolution and frame rate first, then upsample spatially and interpolate temporally. This lets users preview cheaply and only pay for the full render on approval — the single most important cost control.<br><br><strong>Capacity planning:</strong> at minutes per job, throughput is jobs/GPU-hour and queue depth translates directly to wait time. Aggressive admission control, priority tiers, and honest ETAs. Reserved capacity rather than autoscaling, since GPU cold starts plus model loading make reactive scaling useless.<br><br><strong>Quality controls:</strong> per-frame and temporal-consistency moderation (a video can be benign frame-by-frame and problematic as a sequence), plus likeness detection.<br><br><strong>Provenance is mandatory here</strong> — deepfake risk makes C2PA metadata and watermarking a design requirement, not an option. And keep a human review path for flagged content; automated moderation on video is meaningfully weaker than on images.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI coding agent.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Architecture:</strong> repo context layer → agent loop with tools → sandboxed execution → verification → diff presentation for human review.<br><br><strong>Context is the hard problem, not generation.</strong> A real repo does not fit in context. Combine: retrieval over code (embeddings plus symbol search — hybrid matters enormously here, since exact identifier lookup is most of what you need), static analysis for the dependency graph and call sites, recently-edited files, and the specific error or test output. Feed the model the <em>relevant</em> slice, not the repo.<br><br><strong>Tools:</strong> read file, search (grep and semantic), edit with precise anchors, run tests, run the type checker, execute code, git operations. Edits should be surgical patches, not whole-file rewrites — smaller diffs are more reliable and far easier to review.<br><br><strong>Verification is what makes it work.</strong> The agent must run tests and the type checker after each change and iterate on failures. This is a rare domain with a cheap, objective, automatic verifier — exploit it fully. An agent that writes code without running it is a suggestion engine.<br><br><strong>Safety:</strong> sandboxed execution with no network and no credentials; never commit or push without human approval; never modify files outside the working tree; and require review of the diff before merge.<br><br><strong>The failure mode to design against:</strong> confidently wrong edits that pass tests because the tests do not cover the change. Surface which tests actually exercised the modified code, and treat "tests pass" as necessary, not sufficient.<br><br><strong>Measure:</strong> task success rate, diff size, tests-passing rate, human edit rate after acceptance, and cost per task.` },

{ q: 'Design a code generation and review system.', tags: [], a: `<strong>Two distinct systems sharing infrastructure — treat them separately.</strong><br><br><strong>Generation:</strong> retrieval over the repo (hybrid, because exact symbol lookup dominates), style/convention context from neighboring code, and verification via tests and type checking. The context assembly determines quality far more than the prompt.<br><br><strong>Review is the more interesting design problem, because precision is everything.</strong> A reviewer that flags 30 issues of which 3 are real gets ignored within a week — false positives destroy adoption faster than false negatives. Design for precision over recall explicitly.<br><br><strong>Review architecture:</strong> for each changed hunk, retrieve the surrounding context, the callers of modified functions, and the relevant tests. Run deterministic tools <em>first</em> — linters, type checkers, static analysis, security scanners — and let the LLM handle what they cannot: logic errors, missing edge cases, inconsistency with the codebase's conventions, and unclear naming. Never have the LLM re-report what a linter already catches.<br><br><strong>Then verify findings before surfacing them.</strong> A second pass that tries to construct a concrete failure scenario for each claimed issue filters most false positives. Findings without a plausible failure path get dropped.<br><br><strong>Presentation:</strong> anchored to specific lines, severity-ranked, with the reasoning visible so a reviewer can dismiss quickly.<br><br><strong>Measure:</strong> precision on accepted findings, dismissal rate per category (a category with 80% dismissal should be disabled), and whether developers actually act on comments — adoption is the real metric.` },

{ q: 'Design a content moderation system using AI.', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Layered, cheapest-first:</strong> hash matching against known-violating content (PhotoDNA-style, near-zero cost, catches known material) → fast classifiers (small fine-tuned models, milliseconds) → LLM for ambiguous cases → human review queue.<br><br><strong>The economics force this cascade.</strong> At millions of items per day, running an LLM on everything is unaffordable and unnecessary — 95%+ of content is trivially fine. Route only the uncertain middle to expensive review.<br><br><strong>The central design decision is the precision/recall operating point, and it differs per category.</strong> CSAM and violent threats: maximize recall, accept false positives, review everything flagged. Spam: favor precision, since over-blocking legitimate users is costly. Make this explicit per policy category rather than tuning one global threshold — a single threshold across categories is the most common design error.<br><br><strong>Context matters and is frequently missed:</strong> the same text is a slur or a reclaimed term depending on speaker and community; a medical image is clinical or explicit depending on where it appears. Feed the classifier surrounding context, not just the item.<br><br><strong>Human review is part of the system, not a fallback:</strong> queue prioritization by severity and confidence, reviewer wellbeing safeguards for graphic content, and — critically — reviewer decisions feed back as training data. That loop is what improves the system over time.<br><br><strong>Also design:</strong> appeals with a real path to reversal, audit logs for regulatory response, and per-region policy variation. And measure by category, since aggregate accuracy hides that one policy area is failing badly.` },

{ q: 'Design a real-time AI recommendation system.', tags: [], a: `<strong>Classic multi-stage funnel</strong> — candidate generation → ranking → re-ranking — because you cannot score millions of items per request.<br><br><strong>Candidate generation (millions → ~1000):</strong> multiple cheap retrievers unioned — ANN over user/item embeddings (two-tower model), collaborative filtering, trending, recently-viewed-similar. Each covers different failure modes; the union is what gives coverage.<br><br><strong>Ranking (~1000 → ~100):</strong> a heavier model scoring (user, item, context) with rich features. This is where most of the quality lives.<br><br><strong>Re-ranking (~100 → ~20):</strong> business logic — diversity (MMR so the feed is not ten near-identical items), freshness, inventory, promoted content, and fairness constraints.<br><br><strong>The real-time requirements:</strong> features must be fresh — a user's last few actions are the strongest signal available, so you need a streaming feature pipeline and a low-latency online feature store. p99 under ~100ms end to end.<br><br><strong>Where LLMs actually help</strong> (be specific, since this is often hand-waved): generating semantic item embeddings from descriptions to solve cold-start; interpreting natural-language queries; and generating explanations. They are generally too slow and expensive for the ranking hot path itself.<br><br><strong>The critical correctness issue: training/serving skew.</strong> If offline features are computed differently than online ones, your model degrades in ways offline evaluation cannot detect. Share the feature computation code between both paths — this is the single most common cause of "it worked in the notebook."<br><br><strong>Measure online:</strong> offline metrics like NDCG correlate weakly with engagement. A/B test everything.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI-powered email assistant.', tags: [], a: `<strong>Capabilities:</strong> triage and prioritization, summarization of threads, draft replies, extraction of tasks and dates, and search.<br><br><strong>Triage is the highest-value and cheapest feature.</strong> A fine-tuned small classifier over (sender, subject, body, thread history, user's past actions) categorizing into urgent / needs-reply / FYI / ignore. Personalized per user — the strongest signal is what <em>this</em> user has previously replied to or archived. Run on every email; it must be cheap.<br><br><strong>Summarization and drafting</strong> are LLM tasks, invoked on demand rather than on every message, since cost scales with volume.<br><br><strong>Drafting requires personalization to be useful:</strong> few-shot examples from the user's own sent mail, ideally retrieved by similarity to the current thread. A generic-sounding draft is worse than no draft because editing it costs more than writing fresh.<br><br><strong>The security design is the part that matters most, and it is frequently underweighted.</strong> Email is an <em>adversarial</em> input channel — an attacker can send an email containing prompt injection, which your system will process. Assume injection succeeds: the assistant must never send email without explicit user approval, never act on instructions found in email content, and never have credentials it could be talked into using. Treat every message body as untrusted data, clearly delimited.<br><br><strong>Privacy:</strong> email is maximally sensitive. Prefer on-device or in-tenant processing, do not retain content beyond what is needed, and never use customer email for training without explicit opt-in.<br><br><strong>Measure:</strong> triage precision/recall per category, draft acceptance rate, and edit distance on accepted drafts.` },

{ q: 'Design a medical diagnosis assistant.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Frame it correctly first: this is a clinical decision <em>support</em> system, not a diagnosis system.</strong> It surfaces evidence and differentials for a clinician; it does not diagnose. That framing is both the safe design and, in most jurisdictions, the regulatory boundary between a tool and a regulated medical device.<br><br><strong>Architecture:</strong> patient context (EHR, labs, history) + RAG over clinical guidelines, drug databases, and literature → generation with mandatory citations → clinician review. Every output cites a source; uncited claims are suppressed, not shown with a caveat.<br><br><strong>Non-negotiable safety design:</strong> no autonomous action ever. Drug interaction and contraindication checks run as <em>deterministic</em> lookups against a curated database, never as LLM generation — this is exactly the kind of high-stakes exact recall where hallucination is unacceptable and a database is both cheaper and correct. Calibrated uncertainty, with explicit "insufficient information" as a first-class output. Explicit escalation for red-flag symptoms.<br><br><strong>Compliance shapes the architecture:</strong> PHI cannot go into training data you cannot delete from — a decisive argument for RAG over fine-tuning here. Audit trails for every recommendation, BAA-covered infrastructure, access controls, and data residency.<br><br><strong>Evaluation must be clinician-designed and measure harm, not accuracy.</strong> A missed sepsis presentation and a wrong medication dose are not comparable to a low BLEU score. Test on adversarial and rare presentations, and measure automation bias — whether clinicians defer to a wrong suggestion they would have caught unaided. That second-order effect is the real risk: a tool that is right 95% of the time can degrade clinician performance on the other 5% by eroding independent judgment.` },

{ q: 'Design a fraud detection system powered by LLMs.', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Be honest about where LLMs fit.</strong> The detection core should remain gradient-boosted trees or similar over structured features — they are faster, cheaper, better calibrated, and more explainable on tabular signals. LLMs add value in specific places, and claiming otherwise is a design smell.<br><br><strong>Where LLMs genuinely help:</strong> (1) processing <em>unstructured</em> signal — merchant descriptions, support chat transcripts, document verification, listing text; (2) generating explanations for analysts, turning "score 0.87, features X/Y/Z" into a readable case summary; (3) investigating flagged cases as an agent — pulling related transactions, checking external sources, assembling a case file; (4) generating synthetic fraud scenarios for testing.<br><br><strong>Architecture:</strong> real-time scoring (tree model, sub-50ms, on every transaction) → threshold routing → LLM-assisted investigation for the flagged minority → analyst review → decisions feed back as labels.<br><br><strong>The class imbalance point:</strong> fraud is a fraction of a percent, so accuracy is meaningless — a model predicting "not fraud" always scores 99.5%. Use precision/recall at a fixed alert volume, and set the threshold from the business cost asymmetry: a missed fraud costs the loss amount, a false positive costs a blocked legitimate customer and support load.<br><br><strong>Adversarial by nature:</strong> fraudsters adapt, so concept drift is continuous, not occasional. Design for rapid retraining, champion/challenger deployment, and monitoring of score distribution shifts.<br><br><strong>Regulatory:</strong> adverse action decisions often require explainability by law. An LLM narrative is not a substitute for feature attribution from the actual scoring model.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI-powered data extraction pipeline for unstructured documents.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Pipeline:</strong> ingest → classify document type → parse with layout awareness → extract fields against a schema → validate → route by confidence (auto-accept / human review) → persist.<br><br><strong>Parsing is the quality ceiling and where most projects fail.</strong> PDF is a rendering format with no structural notion of tables or reading order. Use layout-aware parsing, and for complex documents render pages and use a vision-language model to transcribe to structured markdown — it handles multi-column flow and merged cells far better than geometric heuristics. Read the extracted text for a sample before building anything downstream.<br><br><strong>Extraction:</strong> define a schema per document type and use <em>constrained decoding</em> against it so invalid output is structurally impossible. Return field-level confidence and source spans — knowing <em>where</em> in the document each value came from is what makes review fast and errors debuggable.<br><br><strong>Validation is what makes this production-viable:</strong> type and format checks, business rules (line items sum to the total, dates are ordered sensibly), and cross-field consistency. Deterministic validation catches a large share of extraction errors for free.<br><br><strong>Human-in-the-loop by confidence:</strong> auto-accept high confidence, route the rest to review with the source span highlighted. Corrections become training data. The economic goal is raising the auto-accept rate over time, so track it as the primary metric.<br><br><strong>Measure per field, not per document.</strong> Aggregate accuracy hides that one critical field is 60% accurate while everything else is 99%. And measure on a human-labeled held-out set — self-evaluation with the same model that extracted is circular.` },

{ q: 'Design a personalized learning assistant.', tags: [], a: `<strong>Core loop:</strong> assess current knowledge → select the next item → teach/explain → check understanding → update the learner model → repeat.<br><br><strong>The learner model is the system, not the LLM.</strong> Maintain a structured, persistent representation: per-concept mastery estimates, misconception flags, pace, and preferred explanation style. Update it from every interaction. Without this, you have a chatbot that forgets — which is what most "AI tutors" actually are.<br><br><strong>Use established pedagogy rather than inventing it:</strong> spaced repetition scheduling for retention, a prerequisite graph over concepts so you never teach something whose foundation is missing, and retrieval practice (testing) over re-reading, which is one of the most robust findings in learning science.<br><br><strong>Where the LLM adds real value:</strong> generating explanations adapted to the learner's level and interests, producing unlimited practice problems at a target difficulty, giving feedback on free-form answers, and — most valuably — <em>diagnosing misconceptions</em> from a wrong answer rather than just marking it incorrect.<br><br><strong>Correctness is a hard constraint:</strong> generated problems and explanations must be verified. For math and code, check answers programmatically. For factual content, ground in a vetted curriculum via RAG. A confidently wrong explanation actively teaches an error — worse than no tutor.<br><br><strong>The design tension to name:</strong> engagement versus learning. Optimizing for session length produces something addictive rather than educational. Measure learning outcomes — retention on delayed tests, transfer to novel problems — not time on platform.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI system for automated code migration.', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Deterministic tools first, LLM for the residual.</strong> AST-based codemods (jscodeshift, OpenRewrite, comby) handle mechanical, pattern-based transformations perfectly, reproducibly, and for free. Use the LLM only where transformation requires semantic understanding the AST cannot express — restructured APIs, changed idioms, logic that must be rewritten rather than rewired. A migration system that reaches for the LLM first is both more expensive and less reliable.<br><br><strong>Pipeline:</strong> analyze the codebase and build a dependency graph → categorize each change site as mechanical or semantic → apply codemods → LLM-transform the remainder with surrounding context and both old and new API documentation → verify → present as reviewable PRs.<br><br><strong>Verification is what makes this tractable:</strong> the existing test suite is the oracle. Type checkers catch a large class of errors immediately. Run both after every file. For a migration without tests, generate characterization tests against the <em>old</em> behavior first — that step is often the real prerequisite, and skipping it makes the whole project unverifiable.<br><br><strong>Ordering matters:</strong> migrate leaves of the dependency graph first, so each change is validated before things depending on it move.<br><br><strong>Chunk into reviewable units.</strong> A 400-file PR gets rubber-stamped. Per-module PRs with the diff and test results get actually reviewed — and human review is not optional here, because the failure mode is subtly changed behavior that compiles and passes existing tests.<br><br><strong>Measure:</strong> percentage auto-migrated, test pass rate, human edit rate per file, and rollback rate — the last one being the honest signal.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI-powered legal document review system.', tags: [], a: `<strong>Use cases differ in shape:</strong> contract clause extraction, obligation and risk identification, deviation-from-standard detection, and due diligence search across a corpus. Design for the specific one rather than "review documents."<br><br><strong>Architecture:</strong> parse with layout preservation (clause structure, numbering, cross-references matter enormously in contracts) → segment by clause rather than by token count → classify clause type → extract structured fields → compare against a playbook of standard positions → flag deviations with citations.<br><br><strong>Clause-level chunking is the key design choice.</strong> Legal meaning is destroyed by arbitrary splits — an obligation separated from its exception or its defined term produces confidently wrong analysis. Preserve the document's own hierarchy, and resolve defined terms as part of preprocessing.<br><br><strong>Non-negotiable requirements:</strong> every finding cites the exact clause and text span, verified to exist. No conclusion without a source. Explicit "unclear / requires attorney review" as a first-class output — the highest-value behavior in this domain is flagging uncertainty, not producing an answer.<br><br><strong>Position it as augmentation:</strong> the system surfaces and prioritizes; the lawyer decides. This is both the safe design and the correct one professionally — unauthorized practice of law is a real constraint, and privilege and confidentiality shape the infrastructure (in-tenant processing, no training on client data, strict access control, audit logs).<br><br><strong>Evaluation:</strong> attorney-labeled sets measuring recall on material issues specifically. A missed indemnity cap matters vastly more than a missed boilerplate notice clause, so weight by materiality rather than reporting flat accuracy.` },

{ q: 'Design a conversational AI with memory across sessions.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Three memory tiers with different access patterns:</strong><br>• <em>Working</em> — current session turns, verbatim, plus a rolling summary.<br>• <em>Semantic facts</em> — durable user attributes in a <em>structured</em> store, always injected regardless of query relevance.<br>• <em>Episodic</em> — past session summaries in a vector index, retrieved by similarity.<br><br><strong>The structured facts store is the design decision that matters</strong> and the one most implementations get wrong by putting everything in a vector database. "User's daughter is named Maya" retrieved only when the query is semantically similar is a failure — critical facts must be unconditionally present.<br><br><strong>Extraction:</strong> after each session, an LLM pass extracts durable facts, with conflict resolution against existing ones. "I moved to Berlin" must <em>supersede</em> the stored city, not coexist with it — so facts need versioning and recency-based resolution, not append-only accumulation.<br><br><strong>Per-turn assembly:</strong> system prompt + all structured facts + top-k episodic memories + recent turns, each with an explicit token budget so no component can starve the others.<br><br><strong>The failure modes to design against:</strong> recursive summarization drift (summarizing summaries compounds loss until facts become confidently wrong — keep extracted facts verbatim); unbounded growth (decay or archive stale episodic memories); and stale facts acted on confidently.<br><br><strong>Privacy is a design requirement:</strong> users must be able to view, edit, and delete what the system remembers. That mandates a real, queryable store with per-item deletion — and is a decisive argument against encoding user specifics anywhere they cannot be removed.` },

{ q: 'How do you design for latency vs quality tradeoffs?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Start from the user's actual latency budget,</strong> which differs by interaction type: autocomplete ~100ms, conversational ~1s to first token, agentic tasks tolerate 30s+ if progress is visible. Design to the budget rather than optimizing generically.<br><br><strong>The available dials, roughly ordered by leverage:</strong> model size (a small fine-tuned model can match a large one on a narrow task); reasoning depth (CoT, self-consistency, reflection each multiply latency); retrieval depth (k, reranking, multi-hop); and output length.<br><br><strong>The highest-value technique is routing, not uniform compromise.</strong> Classify request difficulty and spend accordingly — a cheap fast path for the common case, an expensive path for the hard tail. This beats picking a single middle operating point, because most traffic is easy and a uniform choice over-serves it while under-serving the tail.<br><br><strong>Then attack <em>perceived</em> latency, which is often the real win:</strong> streaming makes a 3-second response feel immediate because TTFT is what users experience. Showing intermediate progress ("searching…", "found 12 sources") transforms a 30-second agent task from broken-feeling to acceptable. Optimistic UI and speculative prefetching hide latency entirely.<br><br><strong>Make the tradeoff measurable:</strong> plot quality against p95 latency for each configuration on your eval set, and choose the knee deliberately. Teams that optimize latency without a quality eval reliably ship something faster and worse, and find out from users.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> quality is not one number. A change that preserves average quality may destroy performance on a critical subset — always check per-category, not just aggregate.` },

{ q: 'How do you implement caching for LLM applications?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Four distinct caching layers, each with different hit rates and risks:</strong><br><br><strong>1. Prompt/prefix caching (highest ROI).</strong> Providers cache the KV state of a static prompt prefix — up to ~90% input cost reduction and lower TTFT. This dictates prompt <em>ordering</em>: static content (system prompt, tool definitions, few-shot examples) first, variable content (user query, retrieved chunks) last. Reordering a prompt for cache-friendliness is often the single cheapest large win available.<br><br><strong>2. Exact-match response cache.</strong> Hash (prompt + model + params) → response. Zero risk, but low hit rate on natural language since users rarely phrase things identically.<br><br><strong>3. Semantic cache.</strong> Embed the query, return a cached response if similarity exceeds a threshold. Much higher hit rate — and genuinely risky. A false hit returns a <em>wrong answer</em> with no error. Use a conservative threshold, and never apply it where small wording differences change the answer ("cancel my order" vs "don't cancel my order" are near-identical embeddings with opposite meanings). Restrict to FAQ-like traffic.<br><br><strong>4. Component caches</strong> — query embeddings, retrieval results, tool outputs within a run.<br><br><strong>The correctness constraints:</strong> key by tenant and permission scope, or one user's cached answer leaks to another. Invalidate on knowledge-base updates. Do not cache personalized or time-sensitive responses.<br><br><strong>Measure hit rate and, critically, cache-hit quality</strong> — sample semantic hits and verify the answer was actually appropriate. An unmeasured semantic cache is a silent quality regression.` },

{ q: 'How do you design rate limiting and cost management for AI APIs?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Rate limit on tokens, not just requests.</strong> This is the key difference from conventional APIs: one request can be 100 tokens or 100k, so request-per-minute limits do not bound cost or capacity. Enforce tokens-per-minute alongside requests-per-minute, and count <em>estimated</em> output tokens at admission rather than only actuals after the fact.<br><br><strong>Layered limits:</strong> per-user, per-organization, per-API-key, and global. Token bucket for smoothing bursts, with a separate concurrency cap — concurrency is what actually protects GPU capacity, since a single user with 200 in-flight long generations can saturate a pool while staying under an RPM limit.<br><br><strong>Cost management:</strong> hard budget caps per tenant checked <em>before</em> each call (estimate from context size plus max output), with tiered behavior — warn at 70%, degrade to a cheaper model at 90%, block at 100%. Post-hoc checking means you have already spent the money.<br><br><strong>Attribution is what makes cost controllable:</strong> tag every call with user, feature, and model, and report cost per feature and per customer. Without attribution you can see the bill but not the cause, and optimization becomes guesswork.<br><br><strong>Watch the distribution, not the mean.</strong> Cost per request is heavily long-tailed — a small number of runaway agent trajectories or huge contexts typically drive most of the spend. Alert on p99, and cap max context and max output as hard rails.<br><br><strong>Degrade gracefully:</strong> queue with backpressure and honest retry-after headers rather than dropping requests, and return a clear error that distinguishes "you are rate limited" from "the service is down."` },

{ q: 'How do you handle failover and fallback for AI systems?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Design a fallback ladder</strong> — each step degrades gracefully rather than failing:<br>1. Retry with exponential backoff and jitter (handles transient errors and rate limits).<br>2. Failover to a different region or deployment of the same model.<br>3. Failover to a <em>different provider</em> — requires abstracting your calls behind a common interface and, critically, having validated prompt behavior on the alternate model in advance.<br>4. Fall back to a smaller or self-hosted model.<br>5. Serve a cached or precomputed response.<br>6. Degrade the feature — return retrieval results without generation, or a templated response.<br>7. Fail honestly with a clear message.<br><br><strong>The step teams skip is validating the fallback.</strong> A prompt tuned for one model often behaves poorly on another, so an untested fallback path silently ships bad output during exactly the incident where you cannot investigate. Run your eval set against every fallback model and keep it in CI.<br><br><strong>Use circuit breakers, not just retries.</strong> When a provider is degraded, aggressive retries make it worse and burn your latency budget. Trip the breaker after a failure threshold, route to the fallback, and probe periodically for recovery.<br><br><strong>Distinguish error types:</strong> rate limits (retry with backoff), 5xx (failover), content filter blocks (do not retry — it will block again), and timeouts (may have partially succeeded, so consider idempotency).<br><br><strong>Test it:</strong> run failure injection regularly. A fallback path exercised for the first time during an outage usually does not work.` },

{ q: 'How do you design an AI system for high availability?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Identify the actual single points of failure,</strong> which for AI systems are usually: one model provider, one region, one vector database, and one GPU pool.<br><br><strong>Provider redundancy</strong> is the distinctive one. Abstract model calls behind a common interface, maintain validated prompts for at least two providers, and keep a self-hosted option for critical paths. Validate the fallback continuously with your eval suite — an untested fallback is not redundancy.<br><br><strong>Statelessness where possible:</strong> inference workers should hold no session state, so any node can serve any request and failure is just a retry. Conversation state, memory, and vector indexes live in replicated stores.<br><br><strong>The GPU-specific problem:</strong> cold starts are minutes because you must load tens of GB of weights. Reactive autoscaling does not work. Keep warm standby capacity, scale on queue depth with lead time, and use admission control rather than accepting unbounded queues.<br><br><strong>Degrade rather than fail.</strong> Availability for an AI system should mean "returns something useful," not "returns the ideal answer." A cached response, a smaller model, or retrieval results without generation all beat a 503.<br><br><strong>Bulkheads:</strong> isolate features so one failing component cannot exhaust shared resources. A misbehaving agent workload should not starve the chat path — separate pools, separate quotas.<br><br><strong>Then measure availability the way users experience it:</strong> not uptime of the endpoint, but the rate of successful, on-SLO responses (goodput). A service returning 200s with degraded quality is not available in any meaningful sense, and only goodput captures that.` },

{ q: 'How do you design a system that degrades gracefully when the model is unavailable?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Decide up front which capabilities are essential and which are enhancements.</strong> That classification is the design — a system where the LLM is load-bearing for everything cannot degrade, only fail.<br><br><strong>The degradation ladder for a typical RAG application:</strong> full generation with retrieved context → smaller/self-hosted model → cached response for similar queries → <em>retrieval results without generation</em> (raw document snippets, which are genuinely useful and require no model at all) → keyword search → static help content with a clear notice.<br><br><strong>The retrieval-without-generation step is the underrated one.</strong> For a documentation assistant, returning the three most relevant passages is perhaps 70% as useful as a synthesized answer and has an entirely independent failure domain. Most teams jump straight from "LLM works" to "error page."<br><br><strong>Be explicit with users.</strong> A degraded response that pretends to be normal destroys trust when it is noticeably worse. "Our AI assistant is temporarily limited — here are the most relevant documents" sets expectations correctly and is far better received than a silently worse answer.<br><br><strong>Preserve the work:</strong> queue requests that cannot be served now and deliver asynchronously where the interaction allows it, rather than discarding them.<br><br><strong>Implementation:</strong> circuit breakers so you fail fast rather than timing out on every request, feature flags to force a degradation level manually during an incident, and per-feature fallback configuration.<br><br><strong>Test it deliberately</strong> — run with the model endpoint blocked in staging, and periodically in production via a canary. Degradation paths that have never been exercised do not work when needed.` },

{ q: 'What are the key considerations for multi-region AI deployment?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Data residency usually dominates the design.</strong> GDPR, and increasingly sector and national rules, may require that data never leave a jurisdiction — which constrains not just where you serve, but which providers you can call and where logs and vector indexes live. This is a hard constraint that shapes architecture, not a deployment detail.<br><br><strong>Model availability differs by region.</strong> Providers do not offer every model in every region, and capacity varies. Your fallback ladder must be region-aware, and you may need different model choices per region — which means your evals must run per region too, since prompt behavior differs across models.<br><br><strong>State replication is the hard engineering problem:</strong> vector indexes are large and expensive to replicate. Options are full replication per region (costly, but low latency and residency-clean), regional partitioning by tenant (usually the right answer for B2B, since customers map to regions), or a primary with read replicas (simplest, but cross-region latency on writes).<br><br><strong>Latency:</strong> route users to the nearest region, but note that inference latency usually dominates network latency — a 100ms cross-region hop matters less than model choice. Do not over-engineer geographic routing at the expense of capacity pooling, since fragmenting GPU capacity across regions raises cost and lowers utilization.<br><br><strong>Consistency:</strong> a document indexed in one region may not be searchable in another immediately. Decide explicitly whether that is acceptable and communicate it.<br><br><strong>Operationally:</strong> per-region monitoring and SLOs, and the ability to fail a region over — tested, not assumed.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI-powered search engine for e-commerce.', tags: [], a: `<strong>Hybrid retrieval is non-negotiable here.</strong> E-commerce queries are heavily lexical — brand names, model numbers, SKUs, sizes — where dense embeddings fail badly, mixed with semantic queries ("warm jacket for hiking"). Run BM25 and vector search in parallel, fuse with RRF.<br><br><strong>Query understanding is where the biggest wins are:</strong> parse the query into structured constraints (brand, category, price range, size, color) plus a free-text component. "red nike running shoes under $100 size 10" should become filters plus a semantic query — treating it as one embedding lookup throws away most of the signal. An LLM or a fine-tuned parser handles this well, and results can be cached by query.<br><br><strong>Ranking is multi-objective, and this is what distinguishes e-commerce from document search:</strong> relevance, plus conversion probability, margin, inventory, shipping speed, and freshness. A learned ranker over these features, with business rules in a re-ranking stage. Pure semantic relevance is not the goal — revenue is.<br><br><strong>Then diversity:</strong> MMR or category-aware re-ranking so the first page is not ten near-identical products.<br><br><strong>Where LLMs add value beyond the query parser:</strong> generating semantic embeddings from product descriptions to solve cold-start for new items; enriching sparse catalog data with inferred attributes; and handling long-tail natural-language queries where no keyword match exists.<br><br><strong>Measure online.</strong> Offline relevance metrics correlate weakly with conversion. A/B test everything, watch null-result rate (a strong signal of query-understanding failure), and monitor zero-click searches per query segment.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI gateway/proxy for managing LLM access across an organization.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>The value proposition is centralizing cross-cutting concerns</strong> that every team would otherwise reimplement badly.<br><br><strong>Core responsibilities:</strong><br>• <em>Auth and attribution</em> — per-team keys mapped to internal identity, with every call tagged by team, application, and user for cost attribution. Without this you get one enormous bill and no idea who spent it.<br>• <em>Unified interface</em> across providers, so switching models is a config change and provider failover is possible at all.<br>• <em>Rate limiting and budgets</em> — token-based, per-team, with tiered degradation before hard blocks.<br>• <em>Routing</em> — by model tier, cost, or a semantic router picking the cheapest capable model.<br>• <em>Caching</em> — prompt caching enforcement and shared response caches across teams.<br>• <em>Guardrails</em> — centralized PII redaction, injection detection, and content filtering, so policy is applied uniformly rather than depending on each team remembering.<br>• <em>Observability</em> — full request/response logging with traces, latency, tokens, and cost.<br><br><strong>The organizational argument that matters most:</strong> centralized logging and policy is what makes an LLM deployment auditable and governable. Security and compliance teams can answer "what data went to which provider" only if there is one path.<br><br><strong>The risks to design against:</strong> the gateway is now a single point of failure on every AI feature — so it needs higher availability than anything behind it, must add minimal latency (streaming pass-through, not buffering), and must not become a bottleneck teams route around. Make the fast path genuinely fast, or shadow deployments will appear.` },

{ q: 'How do you design a RAG system that handles conflicting information?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Classify the conflict type first, because they have different fixes.</strong><br><br><strong>Stale versus current</strong> — the most common, and a data problem rather than a model problem. Fix with versioning: <code>valid_from</code>/<code>valid_until</code>, supersedes pointers, and a default retrieval filter to current versions. If your index serves both the 2023 and 2026 policy with no way to distinguish them, no prompting resolves it correctly.<br><br><strong>Scope difference</strong> — both correct, for different regions, products, or audiences. A metadata problem: tag applicability and filter by user context, or the model is being asked to choose between two right answers.<br><br><strong>Genuine disagreement</strong> — the corpus is actually inconsistent.<br><br><strong>For genuine conflict, do not silently pick one.</strong> Establish a source authority hierarchy in metadata (official policy > wiki > chat export) and instruct the model to prefer higher authority — but <em>surface</em> the disagreement with provenance: "Policy doc (Jan 2026) says X; team wiki (2024) says Y." A confident synthesis that hides a contradiction is the worst outcome, because the user has no way to know and no audit trail.<br><br><strong>Detect conflicts systematically:</strong> run NLI/entailment checks across retrieved chunks to flag contradictions, and route flagged cases to the conflict-presentation path or human review.<br><br><strong>The organizational second-order effect worth naming:</strong> RAG makes existing corpus inconsistency visible for the first time. Feed detected conflicts back to document owners — the durable fix is upstream, and your retrieval system is now the best contradiction detector the organization has.` },

{ q: 'How do you approach capacity planning for an AI system?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Model the load in tokens, not requests.</strong> Requests are a misleading unit because one can be 100 or 100k tokens. Estimate: requests/sec × (average input tokens + average output tokens), and separately track the <em>distribution</em>, since the tail drives capacity.<br><br><strong>Then convert tokens to hardware using the two-phase structure:</strong> prefill throughput is compute-bound (roughly FLOPs-limited, scales with prompt tokens), decode throughput is memory-bandwidth-bound (each step re-reads weights plus KV cache). These size differently, which is exactly why disaggregated serving exists. Benchmark your actual model on your actual hardware at your actual sequence lengths — vendor numbers assume favorable conditions and will mislead you.<br><br><strong>KV cache memory usually sets the concurrency ceiling,</strong> not compute: <code>2 × layers × kv_heads × head_dim × seq_len × batch × bytes</code>. Max concurrent sequences = available VRAM after weights ÷ per-sequence cache. That number, not FLOPs, is typically what limits how many users you can serve.<br><br><strong>Plan for peak, not average.</strong> AI workloads are bursty, and GPU cold starts are minutes — you cannot scale reactively. That forces either over-provisioning (expensive) or admission control and queueing (degraded but bounded). Choose deliberately and communicate the SLO that results.<br><br><strong>For API-based systems,</strong> capacity planning becomes quota negotiation and multi-provider redundancy rather than hardware — but the token modeling is the same.<br><br><strong>Include headroom for retries and fallbacks,</strong> which is where naive plans break: an incident triggers retries that multiply load exactly when capacity is already stressed.` },

{ q: 'Design a multi-tenant AI chatbot platform with custom chatbots per business.', tags: [], a: `<strong>The central question is isolation level, and it is primarily a security decision.</strong><br><br><strong>Data isolation:</strong> namespace or index per tenant in the vector store — shared indexes with a <code>tenant_id</code> filter work but one missing filter is a cross-tenant breach, so if you use them, enforce the filter in a shared data-access layer that cannot be bypassed. Tenant identity always comes from the authenticated session, never from client input or model output.<br><br><strong>Configuration per tenant:</strong> system prompt, branding, knowledge base, enabled tools, model tier, guardrail policy, and escalation rules — all versioned config, not code. This is the product.<br><br><strong>Customization without per-tenant models:</strong> prompt plus RAG covers the large majority. If tenants need genuinely different behavior, use LoRA adapters served against one shared base model with per-request adapter selection — that is what makes hundreds of "custom" models economically viable, versus one full model per tenant, which does not scale.<br><br><strong>Shared infrastructure with per-tenant quotas:</strong> token budgets, rate limits, and concurrency caps so one tenant cannot starve others. Cache keyed by tenant — an unkeyed cache leaks answers across customers.<br><br><strong>Operations:</strong> per-tenant metrics, cost attribution, and logs (tenants will ask what their bot said). Rolling out a prompt or model change requires per-tenant canarying, because a change that improves aggregate quality can break one tenant's specific use case.<br><br><strong>The support burden to plan for:</strong> "our bot gave a wrong answer" requires reproducing with that tenant's config and knowledge base — so store enough trace data to replay any conversation.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI meeting summarizer for thousands of meetings daily.', tags: [], a: `<strong>Pipeline:</strong> audio ingest → ASR with diarization → transcript cleanup → chunked summarization → structured extraction (decisions, action items with owners, open questions) → delivery and indexing for search.<br><br><strong>Batch, not real-time.</strong> Meetings end and summaries are expected within minutes, not milliseconds. That means a queue with worker pools, which lets you use cheaper batch inference tiers and smooth load across the day rather than provisioning for the 9am and 1pm spikes — those spikes are severe in this workload, since meetings start on the hour.<br><br><strong>ASR quality sets the ceiling.</strong> Speaker diarization matters enormously — action items are useless without owners — and domain vocabulary (product names, acronyms, people's names) needs a custom vocabulary or biasing list. Poor transcripts produce confidently wrong summaries with no signal that anything failed.<br><br><strong>For long meetings, map-reduce:</strong> summarize segments in parallel, then synthesize. Verify at the <em>segment</em> level before reduction, since an error introduced in a segment summary is treated as ground truth by the reduce step with no access to the source.<br><br><strong>Structured extraction beats prose summaries in value.</strong> Decisions, action items with owners and dates, and unresolved questions — as structured fields, so they can flow into task systems. Extract with a schema and constrained decoding.<br><br><strong>Hallucination control:</strong> require every action item to cite a transcript timestamp, and verify the span exists. An invented commitment attributed to a named person is the failure mode that destroys trust in this product.<br><br><strong>Privacy:</strong> consent handling, per-meeting access control inherited from the calendar invite, and retention policies.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI notification system that prioritizes instead of broadcasting.', tags: [], a: `<strong>The core function is a per-user relevance and urgency model, not a generator.</strong> Score each candidate notification on: relevance to this user's current work, urgency (is it time-sensitive), actionability (can they do something), and novelty (is this new information).<br><br><strong>Personalization signal:</strong> the strongest predictor is the user's own past behavior — what they opened, acted on, dismissed, or muted. A per-user model over these labels beats any hand-tuned global rules, and it is cheap: this should be a small classifier, not an LLM call per notification, given the volume.<br><br><strong>Where LLMs help specifically:</strong> understanding unstructured content to assess relevance, <em>summarizing and merging</em> related notifications into one digest ("3 updates on the migration project"), and generating the notification text itself so it conveys the actual information rather than "you have an update."<br><br><strong>Design the delivery policy, not just the score:</strong> immediate interrupt only above a high urgency threshold; batched digests at user-chosen times for the middle; and in-app-only for the low tier. Respect quiet hours and per-channel preferences.<br><br><strong>The critical constraint is asymmetric cost.</strong> A false positive (interrupting for something unimportant) is far more damaging than a false negative, because a few bad interrupts train the user to ignore <em>all</em> notifications — and that is unrecoverable. Tune for precision, and bias toward the lower-urgency channel when uncertain.<br><br><strong>Measure the right thing:</strong> action rate per notification and mute/disable rate, not delivery or open rate. Open rate rewards clickbait; action rate and unsubscribes reveal whether the system is actually useful.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI-powered anomaly detection system for cloud infrastructure.', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Statistical and ML methods do the detection; LLMs do the explanation and investigation.</strong> Being clear about this division is what separates a real design from a buzzword answer.<br><br><strong>Detection layer:</strong> per-metric baselines with seasonality (traffic has daily and weekly cycles — a naive threshold alerts every Monday morning), forecasting models (Prophet, SARIMA) for expected ranges, and multivariate methods for correlated signals. Fast, cheap, running on every metric continuously.<br><br><strong>The hard problem is not detection, it is alert fatigue.</strong> A system that flags every statistical deviation is useless. Correlate related anomalies into a single incident, suppress alerts on known maintenance windows, and require sustained deviation rather than single-point spikes.<br><br><strong>Where the LLM earns its place:</strong> once an incident fires, an agent gathers context — recent deploys, config changes, correlated metrics, related logs, similar past incidents — and produces a hypothesis with evidence. That triage work is what takes an on-call engineer 20 minutes, and it is genuinely well-suited to an agent with read-only tools.<br><br><strong>Log analysis</strong> is a second good fit: clustering error messages, detecting novel error patterns, and summarizing a spike into a description.<br><br><strong>Design constraints:</strong> read-only tools for the investigating agent — no remediation actions without human approval. Bounded investigation budget. And the system must work when the LLM provider is down, since that is precisely when you are having an incident.<br><br><strong>Measure:</strong> precision on alerts (the fatigue metric), time-to-detection, and whether the generated hypothesis matched the actual root cause — that last one is the honest test.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI document processing pipeline for financial institutions.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Pipeline:</strong> ingest → classify document type → layout-aware parse → schema-constrained extraction → validation → confidence-based routing (auto-accept / review) → persist with full audit trail.<br><br><strong>Regulatory requirements shape the architecture, not just the policies:</strong> full audit trails (every extraction traceable to a source span and a model version), explainability for decisions affecting customers, data residency, retention schedules, and the ability to reproduce any historical output — which means versioning models, prompts, and code together, and never silently upgrading a model endpoint.<br><br><strong>Accuracy requirements are asymmetric and much higher than typical.</strong> A wrong number in a financial extraction has direct monetary consequence. Design for a high auto-accept threshold and route everything else to humans — the economic goal is raising the auto-accept rate over time, tracked as the primary metric, not maximizing raw accuracy.<br><br><strong>Validation is the highest-leverage component:</strong> deterministic business rules catch a large share of errors for free — line items sum to totals, debits equal credits, dates are ordered, values fall in plausible ranges, cross-document consistency. Run these before human review so reviewers see only genuinely ambiguous cases.<br><br><strong>Extraction:</strong> constrained decoding against a per-document-type schema, with field-level confidence and source spans. Spans are what make review fast and errors auditable.<br><br><strong>Security:</strong> in-tenant or on-prem processing for sensitive documents, encryption, strict access control, and no customer data in training without explicit contractual permission.<br><br><strong>Measure per field</strong> — aggregate accuracy hides that one critical field is failing.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI dynamic pricing engine.', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Be direct: LLMs are largely the wrong tool for the pricing decision itself.</strong> Pricing is a well-studied optimization problem with structured inputs — demand elasticity models, competitor prices, inventory, costs, time-to-expiry. Use econometric models, gradient boosting, or reinforcement learning for the decision. An LLM setting prices is neither more accurate nor explainable, and explainability is a hard requirement here.<br><br><strong>Where LLMs do add value:</strong> extracting competitor prices and product attributes from unstructured sources (listings, scraped pages); matching products across catalogs where descriptions differ; explaining pricing decisions to internal stakeholders; and forecasting demand signal from unstructured text (reviews, news, social).<br><br><strong>Architecture:</strong> feature pipeline (demand, inventory, competitor, seasonality) → elasticity/demand model → constrained optimizer → guardrails → price publication with full audit trail.<br><br><strong>Guardrails are the most important component,</strong> and this is where designs fail: hard floors (never below cost), ceilings, maximum change per period (large swings destroy customer trust), and category-level sanity checks. Automated pricing without bounds produces headline-generating errors — the classic case being algorithmic feedback loops driving prices to absurd values.<br><br><strong>Legal constraints are real and non-obvious:</strong> algorithmic price coordination between competitors is an antitrust risk even without explicit agreement, personalized pricing raises discrimination concerns, and some jurisdictions regulate surge pricing. Build in constraint enforcement and audit logs to demonstrate compliance.<br><br><strong>Measure with controlled experiments</strong> — holdout groups at fixed prices — since observational data cannot separate your pricing effect from demand shifts.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI resume screening system for 100K applications per week.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Lead with the constraint that dominates this design: legal and fairness risk.</strong> Automated employment decisions are regulated — NYC Local Law 144 requires bias audits and candidate notice, the EU AI Act classifies employment screening as high-risk, and US EEOC guidance applies disparate impact analysis. Design as <em>ranking and augmentation</em>, never autonomous rejection.<br><br><strong>Pipeline:</strong> parse resume → extract structured fields (skills, experience, education) → match against structured job requirements → score → rank for human review. Deterministic filters (hard requirements like work authorization or a required license) run first and are auditable.<br><br><strong>Deliberately exclude protected attributes and their proxies</strong> — name, address, photo, graduation year, school prestige, and gaps in employment history. This is the core fairness engineering, and it is harder than removing the obvious fields because proxies are pervasive. An LLM reading a raw resume infers demographics from name, university, and phrasing whether you want it to or not.<br><br><strong>The strongest mitigation is structured extraction before scoring:</strong> extract skills and experience into a schema, then score on <em>that</em>, so the scoring model never sees free-text resume prose. This makes the decision auditable and drastically reduces proxy leakage.<br><br><strong>Bias testing is mandatory and ongoing:</strong> measure selection rates by demographic group on historical data (four-fifths rule as a starting point), test with counterfactual resumes differing only in name, and re-audit after every model change.<br><br><strong>Scale is the easy part</strong> — 100k/week is ~10/sec, comfortably handled with batch processing and a small fine-tuned model rather than a frontier LLM per resume.<br><br><strong>Human review is required</strong> for every rejection, with the reasoning surfaced.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI voice assistant architecture.', tags: [], a: `<strong>Pipeline:</strong> wake word (on-device, always-on, tiny model) → VAD and endpointing → ASR → NLU/LLM → response generation → TTS → audio out. Barge-in support throughout so the user can interrupt.<br><br><strong>Latency is the defining constraint.</strong> Natural conversation requires under ~500ms from end of speech to start of response; beyond ~1s it feels broken. That budget must cover ASR finalization, LLM inference, and TTS first-chunk — so every stage must stream and overlap rather than run sequentially.<br><br><strong>The techniques that make the budget:</strong> streaming ASR that emits partial transcripts, so the LLM can begin prefill before the user finishes speaking; <em>speculative</em> processing on partial transcripts, discarded if the user continues; streaming LLM output into streaming TTS sentence by sentence, so audio starts before generation completes. Sequential stages guarantee failure.<br><br><strong>Endpointing is underrated and a common source of bad UX:</strong> deciding when the user has finished speaking. Too aggressive and you cut people off mid-thought; too lax and responses feel sluggish. Semantic endpointing (is this utterance complete?) beats pure silence thresholds.<br><br><strong>Architecture choice:</strong> cascaded (ASR → LLM → TTS) is debuggable, swappable, and lets you use the best component at each stage. End-to-end speech-to-speech models have lower latency and preserve prosody and emotion, at the cost of controllability and much harder debugging. Cascaded remains the pragmatic default for most products.<br><br><strong>On-device versus cloud:</strong> wake word always on-device; consider on-device ASR for privacy and offline capability, with cloud for the reasoning step.` },

{ q: 'Design a multi-agent workflow system for collaborative complex tasks.', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Start by scoping honestly:</strong> most workflows do not need multiple agents. Multi-agent earns its place when there are genuinely parallelizable subtasks, when context isolation is the binding constraint, or when role separation is the point (generate then independently review). Otherwise a single agent with good tools is cheaper, faster, and vastly easier to debug.<br><br><strong>Architecture:</strong> an orchestrator holding shared state and a task graph → worker agents with scoped tools and isolated contexts → results merged back through defined contracts.<br><br><strong>The design decisions that matter:</strong><br>• <em>Explicit state schema</em>, persisted and checkpointed, separate from any agent's context. This is what makes resumption, human approval pauses, and debugging possible.<br>• <em>Structured handoff contracts</em> — a subagent returns <code>{status, findings[], sources[], confidence, unresolved[]}</code>, not prose. Natural-language handoffs are lossy and unvalidatable, and this is where errors enter.<br>• <em>Global budgets at the orchestrator</em> — per-agent caps sum to unbounded under fan-out, which is how these systems produce surprise bills.<br>• <em>Dependency-aware scheduling</em> — parallelize independent branches, sequence dependent ones.<br><br><strong>The dominant failure mode is error compounding:</strong> a confidently wrong sub-result is consumed as ground truth downstream, with no uncertainty signal. Pass confidence and provenance with every result, and have the merging step flag single-source or low-confidence claims rather than smoothing them into a coherent-sounding whole.<br><br><strong>Observability:</strong> one trace spanning the entire run with parent-child spans. Debugging a multi-agent failure without a unified trace is close to impossible.` },

{ q: 'Design a real-time AI transcription system for concurrent audio streams.', tags: [], a: `<strong>Architecture:</strong> WebSocket/WebRTC ingest → per-stream buffering → VAD to skip silence → streaming ASR on GPU workers → partial and final transcript emission → optional diarization and punctuation → downstream consumers.<br><br><strong>Streaming ASR is the core requirement.</strong> Use a model designed for it (chunked/causal attention, transducer-based) rather than a batch model like vanilla Whisper — batch models need the full utterance and cannot emit useful partials. Emit partial hypotheses continuously and finalize on endpoint, accepting that partials will be revised.<br><br><strong>The scaling insight: VAD is the highest-leverage optimization.</strong> Real conversations are 40–60% silence, so gating GPU work on voice activity roughly doubles capacity for near-zero cost. Do this before anything else.<br><br><strong>Batching across streams</strong> is the second lever — GPU utilization is terrible at batch size 1, so batch audio chunks from many concurrent streams into single inference calls. This requires a scheduler that assembles batches within a tight latency budget (tens of milliseconds), which is the main engineering complexity.<br><br><strong>Capacity planning:</strong> concurrent streams per GPU is the unit, derived from model RTF (real-time factor) after VAD gating and batching. Benchmark on your hardware — vendor RTF numbers assume ideal batching.<br><br><strong>Reliability:</strong> streams are long-lived, so handle reconnection with buffered audio, and checkpoint transcripts so a worker crash does not lose the session. Backpressure when overloaded — degrade to a smaller model or higher latency rather than dropping audio.<br><br><strong>Quality:</strong> domain vocabulary biasing for names and jargon, and diarization if speaker attribution matters, which is expensive enough to make optional per stream.` },

{ q: 'Design an AI-powered live streaming content moderation system.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>The defining constraint is that you cannot review before publication.</strong> Content is already live, so moderation is detect-and-intervene under seconds of latency, not pre-publication filtering. Every design decision follows from that.<br><br><strong>Multi-modal, sampled pipeline:</strong> video frames sampled at a low rate (1–2 fps, not every frame — full-rate analysis is unaffordable and unnecessary), audio transcribed continuously, and chat/text monitored. Each stream feeds fast classifiers.<br><br><strong>Cascade for cost:</strong> cheap classifiers on everything → escalate uncertain cases to heavier models → route to human reviewers. At thousands of concurrent streams, running expensive models on all content is not viable, and the vast majority is benign.<br><br><strong>Intervention ladder rather than binary takedown:</strong> flag for review → reduce discoverability → add a warning interlay → delay the stream (a broadcast delay of even 10–30 seconds converts this into a near-pre-publication problem and is the single most effective control) → cut the stream. Match severity to confidence, and reserve automatic cutting for very high confidence on severe categories.<br><br><strong>Temporal context matters and is frequently missed:</strong> a single frame is often ambiguous while a sequence is clearly violating, and vice versa. Aggregate signals over a window rather than acting on isolated detections.<br><br><strong>Human reviewers are part of the system:</strong> prioritized queues by severity and viewer count, with wellbeing safeguards for graphic content, and their decisions feeding back as training labels.<br><br><strong>Design for appeals and audit</strong> — retain the evidence segment for any enforcement action, since creators will contest and regulators may ask.` },
]};
